  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2,\\,y={t}^{7}+2.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7{t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7{t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-2)={t}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7{t}^{2})}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14t}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=2}=\\frac{14}{(2)^{3}}=\\frac{14}{8}=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$",
